@@ -98,7 +98,18 @@ ### Ticket
 
 **Immutability enforcement:** the write-once rule is enforced on the `Ticket` model (`updating` hook), so it applies to Eloquent saves only — mass updates through the query builder (`Ticket::query()->update(...)`) bypass it and must not be used on issued tickets.
 
-**Single-ticket call (scope note):** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (entity-membership checked, exactly-once). This minimal domain primitive proves the mutable `status`/`called_*` fields; deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) and HTTP/Livewire panels remain out of scope per #14.
+**Single-ticket call:** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (entity-membership checked, exactly-once). It is the low-level primitive; deterministic next-ticket selection is implemented by `CallNextTicket` below.
+
+**Call next selection:** `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` (constructed without arguments) is invoked as `__invoke(Queue $queue, User $calledBy, CallMode $mode = CallMode::Any): Ticket`. `CallMode` is string-backed: `any`, `priority_only`, `normal_only`.
+
+- Only `waiting` tickets of the given queue are candidates; tickets from other queues and already called tickets are never selected.
+- Ordering is deterministic: higher `priority` first, then oldest `issued_at`, then lowest `id`.
+- `any` applies the full ordering plus fairness: if the last three calls in the queue were all priority tickets while a normal ticket is waiting, the call is restricted to normal tickets (oldest first).
+- `priority_only` considers only tickets with `priority > 0`, applies full ordering and ignores fairness.
+- `normal_only` considers only tickets with `priority = 0`, calls the oldest first and ignores fairness.
+- Calls made in any mode count in the fairness history; a normal call resets the priority streak.
+- An inactive queue is rejected with `InactiveQueueException`; a mode with no eligible ticket raises `NoWaitingTicketsException`.
+- Selection runs in a transaction that locks the queue row (reading `is_active` from the database, not a possibly stale model), then locks the chosen ticket row and re-checks that it is still `waiting`, skipping any ticket claimed concurrently by `Ticket::call()`. The winner receives `status=called`, `called_at=now()` and `called_by_user_id`; only those write-once-exempt fields change.
 
